<!--
Behavior Modification Assignment, Handout 3 of 3.

Item 1b (the data) is split into four TextAreas, one per week. On paper this is
scored one point per week present — {{corpus:1c/p1:series:0:32:sha=d1f3e922817c:shape=C4040401}} — so four
fields map exactly onto the four credit slots, and a missing week is visible
rather than buried in a wall of text. Each field holds seven comma-separated
numbers, Sunday through Saturday, because 1c plots them.

ITEM 1c — the graph. Worth 10 of the 40 scored points; the paper item asks for
a graph carrying a title, both axis labels, and a legend. This page draws it:
SelfMonitorPlot reads the four data fields from 1b and the three label fields
in 1c, so the student's own numbers and their own labelling produce their own
chart, live. Printing the review screen yields a submission containing the
figure.

That closes a gap this file previously documented. The earlier version could
only capture the four labelling elements as text and let the LLM check them,
because lo-blocks has no file-upload block and a chart image made in a
spreadsheet could not be submitted here. Capturing labels is NOT a substitute
for a graph: on the paper version one student submitted exactly that — "Title:
{{corpus:1c/p20:title:0:27:sha=d794c8f137de}} / X-axis label: Days / Y-axis label: Hours of
Sleep / Legend: Baseline, Week 1..." — with no plotted data, and was correctly
scored zero. Now the labels and the plotted data are the same artifact, so that
failure mode is not reachable.

Two consequences worth knowing:

  * The legend is the student's to write, like every other label. They name the
    four plotted series in their own box and SelfMonitorPlot keys the chart off
    those names, so until they do, their chart reads "Series 1, Series 2, ..."
    — which is what an unlabelled key actually looks like, and is scored as no
    legend. There is no separate legend-heading box: Observable Plot renders no
    label for a swatches legend, so it would collect text the chart never shows.
    Earlier revisions generated the series names,
    which made the paper item's legend check unscoreable here; it is scoreable
    again.
  * ObservablePlot still renders the worked example in 1c, which is the right
    block for it — fixed author-supplied data. It cannot read student state
    at all: its spec is literal text, and `{{...}}` interpolation exists only
    in Markdown. That asymmetry is why SelfMonitorPlot exists.

The graph is now produced here, from the student's own data. 1c does not offer
building it elsewhere as an alternative: the page draws a correctly-structured
chart from the numbers in 1b, so a spreadsheet detour would only reintroduce
the unlabelled-or-missing-figure failure this item keeps losing points to.

EVERY <LLMAction> PROMPT BODY IN THIS FILE IS GENERATED from `rubric_h3.py` in
~/code/molly_scoring/scorer, following `score.py:build_prompt`'s skeleton so
that the web and the CLI scorer run the SAME rubric. Do not hand-edit one — the
next `--write` overwrites it, and `--check` fails in the meantime:

  ~/code/molly_scoring/scorer/olx_prompts.py --check   # are they current?
  ~/code/molly_scoring/scorer/olx_prompts.py --write   # regenerate

To change what a prompt says, change the rubric. Every difference that remains
between the two implementations is listed, with its reason, in
scorer/EQUIVALENCE.md. As of this revision 1c carries all five of the paper
item's components on the same 10 points, so nothing about its scoring diverges;
what it drops is three guidance bullets describing .docx evidence that does not
exist here.

Item 1b has no LLM check, and does not need one: it is scored a point per week
of data present, which is a property of the fields rather than a judgement about
prose. SelfMonitorPlot performs that check — it reports, under the chart, any
week left empty, any week whose value count is not seven, and any entry it could
not read as a number. Before that existed a student could leave two weeks blank,
get a chart that looked finished with fewer bars, and never be told; on the paper
version two of twenty lost points for exactly that. The other five items each
have their own LLM check, matching the item-by-item scorer in ~/code/molly_scoring.

Canonical wording in each prompt is the scoring dictionary's own phrasing, so a
student who fixes what the LLM flagged meets the same words if the same gap is
graded later. The prompt carries the point values because the rubric does; it
forbids the model from repeating them to the student.

Two calibration findings from the paper corpus reach the prompts as rubric
guidance bullets and should not be tightened back: 1a is graded on the ARC
rather than one sentence per week ("THE BAR IS THE ARC, NOT A SENTENCE COUNT"),
and 2a counts CONTENT rather than sentences — two sentences can satisfy all
three of its slots. Both items were over-strict before that was measured.

Item 1c declares a `slots` sheet for all five of the paper item's components —
having a graph, a title, both axes and the legend, 2 points each — so each gets
a required verdict rather than a mention in prose. The totals agree at 10, and
agreement_app.rebuild_gold_1c rebuilds gold from the grader's own verdicts on
the same 10 rather than rescaling anything. Its verdict sets carry the distinctions
that actually cost points on the paper version: `tick_values` for an axis whose
"label" is really its values ("Sunday, Monday, ..." rather than "Days of the
Week"), which was the single most common deduction, and `generic` for a label
still carrying the example wording. A plain met/absent check cannot express
either, and would credit both.

`generic` is on the title AND the y-axis, because the worked example sits on
this same screen with real labels — 'Water {{corpus:1c/p5:title:5:32:sha=97ca3baaf650:shape=R27-0-27}} and
'Ounces of Water per Day' — three keystrokes from the student's own boxes. On
paper this failure took the form of leaving the template chart in place; here it
takes the form of copying its words, so the prompt names those exact strings.
The x-axis is deliberately exempt: '{{corpus:1c/p2:x:0:16:sha=f1f5ac605348:shape=R12-1-57,R16-0-27}} is the example's label,
the placeholder AND the right answer for nearly everyone, since every week is
plotted Sunday to Saturday. Adding `generic` to y_axis_label is score-neutral —
any verdict but the first already costs the slot's 2 points — so it buys a
better reason in the feedback, not a different score.

The sheet opens with a `!has_own_graph` GATE carrying the paper item's two
whole-item findings, asked of the DATA rather than of a file, because here the
data is what a graph is made of:

  * `absent` = "did not include". Blank or non-numeric weeks in 1b render no
    chart, so there is no graph to label. Deliberately generous — fail it only
    when the data is plainly missing or plainly not numbers, since a whole-item
    gate should not turn on a miscount and SelfMonitorPlot already warns under
    the chart about a short or unreadable week.
  * `mismatch` = TEMPLATE_GRAPH_ONLY. A student can type the worked example's
    OWN numbers into 1b and get the example's chart back, which is the paper
    failure of leaving the template in place, reproduced exactly. The prompt is
    given those numbers so it can recognise them.

That second one is why the 1b placeholders no longer show the example's data.
They used to read "e.g. 8, 10, 8, 12, 6, 10, 0" — the worked example's baseline
week, verbatim — which invited the very copying this now scores. Keep any future
placeholder plainly distinct from the water-drinking numbers.

Both of these are why 1c's prompt is passed the four 1b fields under "Graph
evidence": they are this version's equivalent of the chart XML and embedded
images the CLI scorer digs out of a .docx.
-->

