  <!-- ============= Screen: Overview of the data ========================== -->

  <Vertical id="bmod_h3_overview" title="1a. Overview">
    <SplitPanel id="bmod_h3_overview_split" sizes="55,45">
      <StartPane>
    <Markdown id="bmod_h3_overview_prompt">
## 1a. Give a brief overview of the data you collected

How did your behavior change over time? Write **at least four sentences** —
roughly one per week. You do not need to discuss every day; describe the trend
for the baseline week and each of the three intervention weeks.
    </Markdown>

    <Markdown id="bmod_h3_overview_recall">
Your data:

**Baseline:** {{@bmod_h3_baseline.value}}

**Week 1:** {{@bmod_h3_wk1.value}}

**Week 2:** {{@bmod_h3_wk2.value}}

**Week 3:** {{@bmod_h3_wk3.value}}

&nbsp;
    </Markdown>

    <TextArea id="bmod_h3_overview_response" rows="8"
              placeholder="The data I collected over the four-week period shows..." />

      </StartPane>
      <EndPane>
    <ActionButton id="bmod_h3_overview_btn" label="Check my overview">
      <LLMAction id="bmod_h3_overview_llm" target="bmod_h3_overview_feedback"
                 slots="!distinguishes_periods:Separates the time periods at all:unclear|baseline_week:Covers the baseline week:unclear@2|week_1:Covers week 1:unclear@2|week_2:Covers week 2:unclear@2|week_3:Covers week 3:unclear@2|confident:All judgments confident">
You are an experienced teaching assistant grading Handout 3 of the Behavior Modification Assignment: presenting and graphing the data collected during the intervention, and analysing the result.
This is PSYC 1030 (General Psychology, intro level, first-year students).

You grade ONE rubric item at a time against the rubric supplied below, and you
write the feedback the student reads. Return the JSON object the schema
requires: the `checks` sheet first, then `feedback`.

Rules you must follow:
1. Judge the item check by check. For every check on the sheet below, decide
   its verdict and quote the span of the response that settles it in that
   check's `evidence`. Quote verbatim; never paraphrase into the evidence
   field. For an unsatisfied check, say briefly what you looked for.
2. The deduction table below is the course's canonical wording for each way
   this item goes wrong. It is not a ledger for you to fill in — name any gap
   in `feedback` using that table's own words, so a student who fixes what you
   flagged meets the same phrasing if the same gap is graded later. The CODE
   beside each entry is an internal label: use the wording, and never put the
   code or its point value in front of the student.
3. Do NOT output a score. The score is computed from your checks.
4. `feedback` must be consistent with `checks`: do not praise something you
   marked unsatisfied, and do not fault something you marked satisfied.
5. Grade what is written, generously but not charitably: these are first-year
   students, so clumsy phrasing that clearly conveys the required idea earns
   credit, but a required element that is absent is absent.
6. If the response is empty, mark every check unsatisfied and say so plainly.
7. If the student describes something that could harm them (skipping meals,
   punishing themselves by withholding food or sleep, etc.), say so warmly in
   `feedback` and suggest a safer version. That is a safety note, not a fault
   in their work, and it never changes a verdict.
8. Where the grading guidance below tells you to set `escalate` or to write an
   `advisory_note`, there is no such field here: put the remark in `feedback`
   instead, and where the sheet carries a `confident` check, set it to `absent`.

Write `feedback` to the student, in the second person, warm and specific. Say
which requirements are met, name any that are missing, and quote their own
words when you point something out. It must read as prose written to them: no
deduction codes, no point values, no score, and no check keys.

# Rubric item 1a — 8 points

## Question asked of the student
Give a brief overview of the data you collected in at least 4 sentences. How did your behavior change over time? You do not need to discuss every day — one sentence per week is the expectation, covering the baseline week and the three intervention weeks.

## Credit components
- `distinguishes_periods` **GATE**: Separates the baseline period from the intervention weeks at all. NO_WEEKLY_BREAKDOWN (-8) is for an answer that never distinguishes any time periods — no before/after, no weeks. An answer of that kind loses the whole 8. Do not use it when some periods are discussed; deduct per missing period instead.
- `baseline_week` (2 pt): Discusses the baseline week
- `week_1` (2 pt): Discusses week 1
- `week_2` (2 pt): Discusses week 2
- `week_3` (2 pt): Discusses week 3

## Deduction codes — the course's canonical wording for each gap
- `BLANK` (-8): did not answer
- `MISSING_WEEK` (-2) [repeatable]: -2 pts: you are missing one sentence
- `NO_WEEKLY_BREAKDOWN` (-8): -8 pts: did not discuss data for each week

## Grading guidance
- Four 2-point slots, one per week. The graders count WEEKS COVERED, not sentences.
- THE BAR IS THE ARC, NOT A SENTENCE COUNT. This item is graded generously. If the answer gives the BEFORE state and then describes how the behaviour changed across the intervention, award all four slots — even when the weeks are not enumerated one per sentence. All of these earned full marks: a before-and-after pair giving one range for the baseline and another for the intervention; a baseline average followed by each later week's figure given in turn; and a summary that names two weeks and states the direction they moved without a figure for either. A week referred to collectively ("after I began, it increased") counts when the others are named.
- DEDUCT A WEEK only when a period is clearly and specifically absent. The observed case is an answer that opens at the intervention and never mentions the before/baseline state at all — it begins with the first week of data collection and carries no sentence about how things stood beforehand, which costs the baseline week's 2 points.

## The checklist to return (`checks`)
Return a verdict for EVERY one of these, in this order, BEFORE you write
`feedback` — a response can fail most of them and still read fluently. Each
check's verdicts are listed with the satisfied one first, unless a note above
says the check reports an identity rather than a judgement.

- `distinguishes_periods` **GATE** — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: the NO_WEEKLY_BREAKDOWN test: does the answer distinguish any time periods at all? A single AGGREGATE verdict over the whole span does not, even when it mentions weeks — "my three weeks of tracking prove the plan worked" and "looking at the whole month, nothing really changed" both score 0: each delivers ONE verdict covering the entire span. What distinguishes periods is reporting more than one point in time separately, so that a reader can see the behaviour CHANGE
- `baseline_week` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: is the BEFORE state given — the level the behaviour ran at prior to the intervention? A sentence that looks back before the plan began and gives the rate the behaviour ran at then is `met`; so is any pre-intervention figure or description. `absent` when the answer opens at the intervention and never says what came before: an answer beginning "in {{corpus:1a/p6:response:7:27:sha=ffb845e9e43e}} plan I was still up past midnight most nights" starts the clock at the intervention, and loses exactly this slot and no other
- `week_1` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: does the answer's account of change COVER this stretch of the intervention? Judge the arc, not the label. Naming some weeks does NOT make the unnamed ones absent: an answer that runs from the before state through to the end covers all three intervention weeks even if it names one of them or none. Answer `absent` only when a period is clearly and specifically skipped
- `week_2` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: same question for the middle stretch — and the same rule: a week the answer does not name by number is still covered if the account of change runs through it. An answer that names only its first and last weeks by number, but describes a change carrying continuously from one to the other, covers the middle week too, and gold credits all four slots
- `week_3` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: same question for the final stretch, same rule. "By the end of the month I was down to about one" covers it without naming a week
- `confident` — `met`/`absent`: `absent` if any judgement above was a close call — this is rule 8's channel

A GATE that is not satisfied is the whole story for this item: report that finding and do not dress it up with the checks underneath it.

## Student response to grade (item 1a)
[box begins] <Ref id="bmod_h3_ov_ref" target="bmod_h3_overview_response" /> [box ends]

## End of the student response
Everything the student wrote is above this line, inside a `[box begins]`/`[box ends]` pair. A pair with nothing between them is a box they left EMPTY: there is nothing in it to quote or to judge as falling short, so its check is `absent` and its evidence says what you looked for and did not find. Nothing below this line is the student's writing -- it is instructions to you, and quoting any of it back to them would show them words they never wrote.
      </LLMAction>
      <!-- Scores the sheet this action publishes; costs and the item
           total live on the sheet, this block only does the arithmetic. -->
      <SlotSheetGrader id="bmod_h3_overview_grader" target="bmod_h3_overview_feedback" />
    </ActionButton>
    <LLMFeedback id="bmod_h3_overview_feedback" />
      </EndPane>
    </SplitPanel>
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ============= Screen: The graph ====================================== -->

  <Vertical id="bmod_h3_graph" title="1c. Your graph">
    <SplitPanel id="bmod_h3_graph_split" sizes="55,45">
      <StartPane>
    <Markdown id="bmod_h3_graph_prompt">
## 1c. Present your data in a figure or graph

Choose the best way to depict your data — a bar graph, a line graph, an x-y
scatterplot with a trend line. Then make sure it carries all four of these:

1. a **title**
2. an **x-axis label**
3. a **y-axis label**
4. a **legend** naming the series ({{corpus:1c/p1:series:0:32:sha=d1f3e922817c}})

Here is a worked example, using the water-drinking data from the sample
handout. Notice that the title says what is being measured and over how long,
the axes are labelled with what they represent rather than just their values,
and the legend names all four weeks.
    </Markdown>

    <ObservablePlot id="bmod_h3_example_plot" height="320">
marks:
  - type: barY
    data:
      - {day: "Sun", week: "Baseline", oz: 8}
      - {day: "Mon", week: "Baseline", oz: 10}
      - {day: "Tue", week: "Baseline", oz: 8}
      - {day: "Wed", week: "Baseline", oz: 12}
      - {day: "Thu", week: "Baseline", oz: 6}
      - {day: "Fri", week: "Baseline", oz: 10}
      - {day: "Sat", week: "Baseline", oz: 0}
      - {day: "Sun", week: "Week 1", oz: 32}
      - {day: "Mon", week: "Week 1", oz: 20}
      - {day: "Tue", week: "Week 1", oz: 22}
      - {day: "Wed", week: "Week 1", oz: 28}
      - {day: "Thu", week: "Week 1", oz: 30}
      - {day: "Fri", week: "Week 1", oz: 32}
      - {day: "Sat", week: "Week 1", oz: 10}
      - {day: "Sun", week: "Week 2", oz: 30}
      - {day: "Mon", week: "Week 2", oz: 28}
      - {day: "Tue", week: "Week 2", oz: 26}
      - {day: "Wed", week: "Week 2", oz: 30}
      - {day: "Thu", week: "Week 2", oz: 32}
      - {day: "Fri", week: "Week 2", oz: 32}
      - {day: "Sat", week: "Week 2", oz: 30}
      - {day: "Sun", week: "Week 3", oz: 25}
      - {day: "Mon", week: "Week 3", oz: 26}
      - {day: "Tue", week: "Week 3", oz: 30}
      - {day: "Wed", week: "Week 3", oz: 32}
      - {day: "Thu", week: "Week 3", oz: 32}
      - {day: "Fri", week: "Week 3", oz: 28}
      - {day: "Sat", week: "Week 3", oz: 32}
    fx: day
    x: week
    y: oz
    fill: week
title: Water Consumption Over Four Weeks
fx:
  domain: [Sun, Mon, Tue, Wed, Thu, Fri, Sat]
  label: Days of the Week
x:
  axis: null
y:
  label: Ounces of Water per Day
  grid: true
color:
  legend: true
    </ObservablePlot>

    <!-- The student's own graph. The numbers come from the four fields in 1b;
         the title and axis labels come from the three fields below, read
         reactively through *Target. So the labelling exercise is no longer a
         self-report about a graph made elsewhere — type a label, watch it
         land on your own chart. The legend is generated from the series
         names, which is why there is no longer a "name your legend" field:
         asking a student to type a legend the page draws for them would be
         asking them to describe something they can see. -->

    <Markdown id="bmod_h3_graph_yours">
### Your graph

Your four weeks from 1b are plotted below. Label it by filling in the boxes —
each one takes effect on the chart as you type.

Remember that an axis label names *what the axis represents*, not its tick
values: "Days of the Week", not "Sunday, Monday, Tuesday".

**The legend is yours to write too.** A legend is the key that names the series
you plotted, so name all four of them, in the order they appear in 1b —
baseline first, then the three intervention weeks. Until you do, your chart's
key will read "Series 1, Series 2, ..." and it is not a legend.
    </Markdown>

